Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "for every offset k there is one fixed matrix M\:2096 (a rotation in each \
pair of components) with PE(pos + k) = M\:2096 PE(pos) for all positions: the \
reason the paper gives for choosing sinusoids"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"78b9b416-6b0a-4521-8f2e-73270214f884"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "the dot product PE(pos)\[CenterDot]PE(pos + 7) is the same for every pos: \
it depends only on the distance"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"b117eff9-1806-4fce-adfd-08c4c1531451"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "as a net (SequenceIndicesLayer, a frozen LinearLayer, Sin) it adds exactly \
these codes, for any length"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8c2bbf70-4585-45d0-926a-28aa6b303b85"],

Cell["\<\
Frozen means LearningRateMultipliers -> 0: training never changes those \
weights.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"a1d0ee3b-3b46-4371-9cc3-ad114109d629"],

Cell["6. The position-wise feed-forward network", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"75473b07-1000-436f-bdc0-86fcd10cb3ca"],

Cell["\<\
After attention, every position goes through the same two-layer network on \
its own: dmodel \[RightArrow] dff \[RightArrow] dmodel with a ReLU between \
(dmodel = 512, dff = 2048 in the base model; Section 3.3).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"6a44b962-1f89-4de3-995c-47350af4a6b6"],

Cell["FFN(x) = max(0, x W\:2081 + b\:2081) W\:2082 + b\:2082", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"4934934b-4c0c-4be0-8d81-fa20e0ca8a40"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "NetMapOperator[NetChain[{LinearLayer[64], Ramp, LinearLayer[16]}]] equals \
the formula at every position"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"d2f1c099-5162-4333-89f3-9c0ebd9f281c"],

Cell["7. Residual connection and layer normalisation", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"aec0a312-7831-4945-bcb5-ac827a4e0268"],

Cell["\<\
Each sub-layer (attention or feed-forward) is wrapped in a residual \
connection and followed by layer normalisation (Section 3.1). The residual \
lets the signal and its gradient pass straight through; the normalisation \
keeps every position's vector at mean 0 and variance 1 before a learned scale \
and shift.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"4bbfe446-d2ff-4e6f-9448-ca12f89bee58"],

Cell["\<\
output = LayerNorm(x + Sublayer(x))
LayerNorm(v) = \[Gamma] (v \[Minus] mean v)/\[Sqrt](var v + \[Epsilon]) + \
\[Beta]\
\>", "Program",ExpressionUUID->"c6aded2a-9314-44bb-bd48-b3b388400276"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["PASS  ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0.5, 0]],
 "NormalizationLayer[2, 2] is LayerNorm over each position's features, with \
\[Epsilon] = 0.001"
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"067d52fb-60d5-4047-90a6-218ab8f2facd"],

Cell["\<\
The paper places the normalisation after the residual sum (post-norm). Many \
later transformers place it before the sub-layer (pre-norm), which is easier \
to train when there are many layers; the lessons follow the paper.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"bdb5b740-c6cf-4fd0-8b82-94a8e9300cb4"],

Cell["8. One encoder layer and one decoder layer", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"67e87bd6-e5c4-43dc-a1ed-a1dfcbfc26ba"],

Cell["\<\
An encoder layer is self-attention, then the feed-forward network, each \
wrapped as LayerNorm(x + Sublayer(x)). A decoder layer has three sub-layers: \
causal self-attention over the output so far, attention over the encoder's \
output (queries from the decoder, keys and values from the encoder), and the \
feed-forward network. The base model stacks 6 of each (Section 3.1).\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"15c2e14b-04c4-4385-ae16-3619bedcee44"],

